A twist of fate brought us together.

37
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,980
I crashed into your life and you into mine.

38
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:42,500
And this may sound crazy, but I'm falling in love with you, Veronica.

39
00:01:57,610 --> 00:01:59,810
Wow, such a romantic dialogue.

40
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,470
It really sounds like they're falling in love.

41
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:11,290
Yeah, it's a really romantic thing that's going on right now, but there were some really useful words here, so why don't we take a look at language takeaway?

42
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:14,340
Language takeaway.

43
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,080
Today we have three words for language takeaway.

44
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:20,760
And the first one is relieved.

45
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:22,360
Relieved.

46
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:23,360
Relieved.

47
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:29,490
So, we have some great examples on how we use relieved and I'm really sure you can understand the meaning from the sentences.

48
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:34,240
Example one.

49
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:41,680
They were relieved to hear that the company would not be making any layoffs.

50
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,040
Example two.

51
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:46,680
I'm so relieved to hear your voice.

52
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:48,780
I was worried something happened to you.

53
00:02:52,230 --> 00:03:00,110
I guess relieved is like a happy or relaxed feeling that you get if you avoid something bad, right?

54
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:01,520
Right.

55
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:02,590
I'm so relieved that didn't happen.

56
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:04,120
Yeah.

57
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:06,480
Now for our second word, pick me up.

58
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:07,260
Pick me up.

59
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:08,940
Pick me up.

60
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:13,000
So when you ask someone to pick you up, you're saying basically...

61
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:13,390
Come and get me.

62
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,430
Okay, so let's give a couple of examples.

63
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:21,290
My husband was supposed to pick me up from work yesterday, but he forgot.

64
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:23,430
Does that really happen?

65
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:23,990
Yes.

66
00:03:25,170 --> 00:03:26,930
All right, we'll have a talk with him.

67
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:29,510
Or we could also say, oh, I need to hurry up.

68
00:03:29,730 --> 00:03:31,270
The taxi will pick me up at seven.

69
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:32,880
Yes.

70
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:33,750
So come and get me.

71
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:35,280
Right.

72
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:36,280
Pick me up.

73
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:39,640
And our final word in today's language takeaway is...

74
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:39,870
Ordinary.

75
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:41,460
Ordinary.

76
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:42,600
Ordinary.

77
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:43,360
Ordinary.

78
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,640
So, ordinary is a normal word.

79
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:48,360
Yes, meaning?

80
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:49,480
It's normal.

81
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,280
Uh-huh.

82
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:52,280
Nothing uncommon about it.

83
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:53,280
Yeah.

84
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:54,320
So you can have like an ordinary person.

85
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:55,020
An ordinary day.

86
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,320
An ordinary day.

87
00:03:57,320 --> 00:03:57,500
Ordinary life.

88
00:03:58,320 --> 00:03:59,320
Uh-huh.

89
00:03:59,320 --> 00:03:59,720
Nothing exciting about it.

90
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:01,660
What else is ordinary?

91
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:02,660
Ordinary podcast.

92
00:04:03,420 --> 00:04:05,640
An ordinary podcast, which English pod is not.

93
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:06,640
Is not.

94
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:07,640
Okay.

95
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:08,640
Yeah.

96
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:09,640
So ordinary.

97
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:10,640
Okay, great words.

98
